    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d870">

                <pb facs="1168" n="1048" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg1048"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.51/881b</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chief of the Division of Latin American
                        Affairs, Department of State</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">White</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Representative on Special Mission in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Crowder</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-11-02"><hi rendition="#italic">November 2,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear General Crowder</hi>: I am just in receipt of a
                    letter from Mr. Blair of Lee, Higginson and Company stating that they are very
                    much interested in the Cuban loan, and that they hope nothing will come up which
                    will prevent them from bidding. Mr. Blair inquires whether the bankers will all
                    bid on the same form of bond to be decided upon by the Cuban Government prior to
                    receiving the bids, or whether each banker will be free to suggest the form of
                    bond he desires to bid on. I told Mr. Blair that so far as I knew this point had
                    not been determined by the Cuban Government. Can you give me any information on
                    this subject?</p>
                <p>With kindest regards [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Francis White</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
